{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788176801,"nonce":"7ahwh3tNOgocsP561PJv5fZlEfM=","message":"RESOLVED at dispatch — the `area` verbs are split between 3.2 and 3.4.\nOrchestrator, session 6, BORGES.\n\n3.2's scope says `area allowed create/name/assign pawn`; 3.4's says\n`allowed area assign (2.3's areas)`. Both claimed the assignment. Settled by\nlooking at where the GAME puts each control rather than by taste:\n\n- **3.2 owns the area ITSELF** — `area home add/remove --rect`,\n  `area allowed create/delete/rename`, and painting or clearing an\n  `Area_Allowed`'s cells over a rect. That is `AreaManager` plus `Area` cell\n  writes: the `Dialog_ManageAreas` and area-designator surface, the same\n  drag-a-rect vocabulary as the rest of 3.2.\n- **3.4 owns assigning a PAWN to an area** — `Pawn_PlayerSettings\n  .AreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap`, a pawn field driven by\n  `PawnColumnWorker_AllowedArea` in the Assign tab, in the same column strip\n  as `medCare`, `hostilityResponse` and `selfTend`, all three of which are\n  already 3.4's scope. It carries its own widget gate to reproduce and cite\n  (`SupportsAllowedAreas` and `pawn.Faction == Faction.OfPlayer`), and the\n  setter re-checks the pawn's current job against the new area.\n\nSo 3.2 creates and shapes areas; 3.4 puts pawns in them. Neither builds the\nother half, and the loop closes when both merge.\n\nBoth workers were told this directly, mid-run, before either wrote the code.","files":null}]}